DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These  functions  convert  network  numbers between presentation (i.e.,
       printable) format and network (i.e., binary) format.

       For both functions, _a_f specifies the address family for the conversion;
       the only supported value is AAFF__IINNEETT.

   iinneett__nneett__ppttoonn(())
       The iinneett__nneett__ppttoonn() function converts _p_r_e_s,  a  null‐terminated  string
       containing an Internet network number in presentation format to network
       format.   The result of the conversion, which is in network byte order,
       is placed in the buffer pointed to by _n_e_t_p.  (The _n_e_t_p  argument  typi‐
       cally  points  to  an _i_n___a_d_d_r structure.)  The _n_s_i_z_e argument specifies
       the number of bytes available in _n_e_t_p.

       On success, iinneett__nneett__ppttoonn() returns the number of bits in  the  network
       number field of the result placed in _n_e_t_p.  For a discussion of the in‐
       put presentation format and the return value, see NOTES.

       _N_o_t_e: the buffer pointed to by _n_e_t_p should be zeroed out before calling
       iinneett__nneett__ppttoonn(),  since  the  call writes only as many bytes as are re‐
       quired for the network number (or as are explicitly specified by _p_r_e_s),
       which may be less than the number of bytes in a  complete  network  ad‐
       dress.

   iinneett__nneett__nnttoopp(())
       The  iinneett__nneett__nnttoopp() function converts the network number in the buffer
       pointed to by _n_e_t_p to presentation format; _*_n_e_t_p is  interpreted  as  a
       value in network byte order.  The _b_i_t_s argument specifies the number of
       bits in the network number in _*_n_e_t_p.

       The  null‐terminated presentation‐format string is placed in the buffer
       pointed to by _p_r_e_s.  The _p_s_i_z_e argument specifies the number  of  bytes
       available  in  _p_r_e_s.  The presentation string is in CIDR format: a dot‐
       ted‐decimal number representing the  network  address,  followed  by  a
       slash, and the size of the network number in bits.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On  success,  iinneett__nneett__ppttoonn() returns the number of bits in the network
       number.  On error, it returns -1, and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the  er‐
       ror.

       On  success,  iinneett__nneett__nnttoopp() returns _p_r_e_s.  On error, it returns NULL,
       and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

